<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heimskringla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1230</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heimskringla is a collection of sagas of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> written in Old Norse around 1230, usually attributed to the Icelandic historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>. Beginning with the legendary Yngling kings, it runs through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Saint Olaf</a> to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson">Magnus Erlingsson</a> in 1177. It is a central, if often legendary, source for Viking-Age and medieval Scandinavian history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heimskringla.jpg" alt="Heimskringla" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National and University Library of Iceland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lbs_fragm_82,_0001r_-_0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Heimskringla was written in Old Norse in Iceland. No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> attributes it to an author, and some scholars assume it was written about 1230 by the Icelandic knight, poet and historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a> (1178/79–1241). The first surviving works that credit him are 16th-century Danish translations by the Norwegians Peder Claussøn Friis and Laurents Hanssøn. The title was first used in the 17th century and comes from the opening words of one manuscript, kringla heimsins, &quot;the circle of the world&quot;. Its sources include earlier kings' sagas such as Morkinskinna and Fagrskinna, 12th-century Norwegian histories and oral traditions, notably skaldic poems.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work is a collection of sagas about Swedish and Norwegian kings. It opens with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglinga-saga">Ynglinga saga</a>, the mythological prehistory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglings">Ynglings</a>, who trace their line to Freyr and the people who came with Odin from Asgard. The later sagas are devoted to single rulers, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-the-black">Halfdan the Black</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, and fable and fact intermingle at first, the accounts becoming more reliable as the collection proceeds. The saga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf II of Norway</a> is the central part, his reign of fifteen years taking up about a third of the whole.</p><p>The saga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> follows his expedition to the East, his time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stamford-bridge">Battle of Stamford Bridge</a> in 1066, a few days before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> fell at Hastings. The saga of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader">Sigurd the Crusader</a> tells of a Norwegian voyage as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. The narrative runs to 1177.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Until the mid-19th century historians put great trust in the factual truth of the sagas. Early in the 20th century saga criticism, pioneered by the Swedish historians Lauritz and Curt Weibull, pointed out that the work was written several centuries after most of the events it describes. The Norwegian historian Edvard Bull said that all illusions had to be given up that it bore a deeper resemblance to what actually happened. It is still used as a source, with caution, and its content is judged more credible for recent times.</p><p>Later Icelandic writing about Scandinavian kings was based on it. The first complete translation, into Danish, was made around 1600 by Peder Claussøn Friis and printed in 1633, and Samuel Laing's English version appeared in 1844. In the 19th century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> gained independence, it became an important national symbol, and in 1900 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> subsidised new translations into both written forms of Norwegian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heimskringla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of what is known about early Scandinavia comes from the kings' sagas written in Iceland, above all Snorri Sturluson's <b>Heimskringla</b>, composed around 1230, while the Norwegian court sponsored translations of French romances and produced the mirror for princes Konungs skuggsjá.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In his <b>Heimskringla</b>, the history of the kings of Norway, Snorri presented Odin as a human king from Asia later worshipped as a god.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Ari Thorgilsson wrote the Book of the Icelanders around 1130, the Book of Settlements recorded the families who settled the island, and Snorri Sturluson wrote the Prose Edda and <b>Heimskringla</b>, the history of the kings of Norway, in the early 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Heimskringla</b> says that Kjotve the Rich, king of Agder, joined the western coalition defeated by Harald Fairhair at Hafrsfjord, traditionally around 872.</blockquote><a href="/state/agder">Agder</a></li><li><blockquote>According to <b>Heimskringla</b>, its king Audbjørn fell fighting Harald Fairhair at the Battle of Solskjel, and the district then passed under Harald's earls.</blockquote><a href="/state/firdafylke">Firdafylke</a></li></ul></section>
